1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,096
Good evening from the OROS closing desk.

2
00:00:03,096 --> 00:00:08,978
This morning the system named no pick, so tonight there is nothing to grade.

3
00:00:08,978 --> 00:00:13,700
No setup, no trade, no loss — that is the discipline working.

4
00:00:13,700 --> 00:00:18,619
Why we grade in public: a forecast that is never checked is just content.

5
00:00:18,619 --> 00:00:24,009
Every morning call gets an evening verdict, and both go in the permanent record.

6
00:00:25,640 --> 00:00:29,381
On the day, the book is down 189 dollars.

7
00:00:29,381 --> 00:00:32,758
Maplebear (CART) leads, +0.7 percent.

8
00:00:32,758 --> 00:00:36,316
Arch Capital Group (ACGL) lags at -1.5.

9
00:00:36,316 --> 00:00:41,244
The checklist is clear: nothing requires action today.

10
00:00:41,244 --> 00:00:43,862
Today's lesson is noise versus signal.

11
00:00:43,862 --> 00:00:47,514
Daily moves are weather; the weekly trend is climate.

12
00:00:47,514 --> 00:00:50,546
The system never exits on a scary afternoon.

13
00:00:50,546 --> 00:00:56,058
It acts only on weekly closes, because that is where the real information lives.

14
00:00:57,760 --> 00:01:05,316
And here is the book itself — the actual holdings, identical to this morning's emailed report.

15
00:01:05,316 --> 00:01:15,203
6 positions, led by Apple, ticker AAPL, Automatic Data Processing, ticker ADP, Coca-Cola Europacific Partners, ticker CCEP.

16
00:01:15,203 --> 00:01:26,135
Every line carries its share count, its entry, and its exit — because a position without a defined exit is not a position, it is a hope.

17
00:01:27,840 --> 00:01:32,642
Now the part that makes this system different: the loop.

18
00:01:32,642 --> 00:01:35,043
Every morning OROS proposes.

19
00:01:35,043 --> 00:01:45,589
Every evening it grades its own call, journals the verdict, and adjusts its own risk before it is allowed to propose again.

20
00:01:45,589 --> 00:01:49,533
Inside the book today: 1 positions up, 5 down.

21
00:01:49,533 --> 00:01:51,934
Best, CART at +0.67 percent.

22
00:01:51,934 --> 00:01:53,649
Worst, ADP at -1.64.

23
00:01:53,649 --> 00:01:58,622
Both go in the journal — wins and losses get the same ink.

24
00:01:58,622 --> 00:02:05,825
The honest scoreboard reads: expectancy -2.75% per trade over 1337 graded positions.

25
00:02:05,825 --> 00:02:12,856
Because that record is soft, the system has throttled itself to 50% position size.

26
00:02:12,856 --> 00:02:18,001
Nobody told it to — full size must be earned back with wins.

27
00:02:18,001 --> 00:02:30,348
What tonight's read hands to tomorrow's open: 156 names close the day in Stage 2; position size stays cut to 50% until the record earns it back.

28
00:02:31,920 --> 00:02:34,241
Over to the sports desk.

29
00:02:34,241 --> 00:02:43,817
Day 98 of the race: three books, the same one hundred thousand simulated dollars, since July sixth.

30
00:02:43,817 --> 00:02:50,298
In first place, the live self-rebuilding book, in blue, at 107,567.

31
00:02:50,298 --> 00:02:57,746
Then the frozen first portfolio, the green line, 9.6 thousand back at 97,996.

32
00:02:57,746 --> 00:03:03,356
Then the index fund, in gray, 9.8 thousand back at 97,734.

33
00:03:03,356 --> 00:03:06,064
The race has no finish line.

34
00:03:06,064 --> 00:03:10,513
Same books, same dollars, back at it tomorrow.

35
00:03:10,513 --> 00:03:15,543
Every line starts the same day with the same dollar.

36
00:03:15,543 --> 00:03:17,478
Nothing is restated.

37
00:03:17,478 --> 00:03:26,348
On screen, three equity lines grown from the same starting dollar: the frozen first book, the live book, and the index fund.

38
00:03:27,720 --> 00:03:35,040
All figures are simulated paper trading, graded and journaled by the OROS agents with no human intervention.

39
00:03:35,040 --> 00:03:37,277
Nothing here is financial advice.

40
00:03:37,277 --> 00:03:40,259
From the closing desk — see you at the open.
